            <ArticleTitle>Complications of fascia lata harvesting for ptosis surgery.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>581-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIMS/BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The aim of this study was to assess the morbidity associated with harvesting autogenous fascia lata for brow suspension ptosis surgery.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A retrospective study by postal questionnaire of 24 consecutive patients.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Early postoperative problems with pain on walking (67%), limping (38%), and wound pain (57%) occurred mostly for less than 1 week. The final cosmetic appearance of the scar caused minor concern in 38% of patients.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Fascia lata is the preferred material for permanent ptosis correction when a brow suspension is required. Most of the patients, following fascia lata harvest, experienced some symptoms of leg pain and limping for less than 1 week. The only long term problem was the scar. 38% of patients found the final cosmetic appearance caused minor concern.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
